WEBVTT

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:09.400
Our world is not always the same.

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:14.160
Hidden from our view lies a different world.

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:16.700
Creatures utterly unlike us.

00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:21.930
Almost alien.

00:00:21.930 --> 00:00:25.220
Yet they are more numerous than any other group on the planet.

00:00:30.440 --> 00:00:40.220
Welcome to the fascinating world of the arthropods: spiders, scorpions, and insects.

00:00:40.220 --> 00:00:46.380
Today we have new camera techniques that will allow us to reveal in greater detail than

00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:53.430
ever before their lives, the way they fight and feed and reproduce.

00:00:53.430 --> 00:01:00.000
This series uses specially developed 3D camera technology to study the micro world in extraordinary

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:06.320
detail both on location and in specially constructed environments. We'll witness

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:10.820
their births, the challenges they face and the moments when their lives hang in

00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:18.160
the balance. And that may help us understand how it is that today over 80%

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:25.680
of all animal species on this planet are arthropods. In this series we'll see the

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:30.000
way they have evolved from the comparative simplicity of the

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:35.460
millipede to vast colonies that contain hundreds, even millions of individuals.

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:41.950
We'll witness the most extraordinary transformations in the animal kingdom.

00:01:41.950 --> 00:01:52.700
We'll meet ants that farm, spiders that can cast their webs, and the bug that wears the

00:01:52.700 --> 00:01:54.820
bodies of its victims as a disguise.

00:01:57.440 --> 00:02:00.000
Welcome to a strange and dangerous world.

00:02:17.020 --> 00:02:21.820
Our planet's rainforests are home to millions of animal species.

00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:25.580
Nearly 90% of them are arthropods.

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:30.000
Some call them bugs, and they're all searching for food.

00:02:30.440 --> 00:02:33.880
In fact, there's food for most of them everywhere,

00:02:33.880 --> 00:02:42.410
because over half of all bugs eat plants.

00:02:42.410 --> 00:03:00.000
Millipedes were among the first arthropods to move up out of the water

00:03:00.090 --> 00:03:07.640
onto the land. They can have up to 750 legs, grow to lengths of 30 centimeters and live

00:03:07.640 --> 00:03:15.020
for as long as seven years. And this body plan is so efficient that it's remained largely

00:03:15.020 --> 00:03:24.060
unchanged for hundreds of millions of years.

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:29.860
Their body is divided into segments, each with its own set of legs and internal organs.

00:03:30.280 --> 00:03:37.970
A heart runs the entire length of it, supplying each segment with oxygen.

00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:42.740
It eats decaying leaves and plants,

00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:46.680
just as its ancestors did over 400 million years ago.

00:03:52.370 --> 00:03:58.480
Millipedes are peaceful creatures, but they don't have the forest to themselves.

00:04:01.560 --> 00:04:06.860
There are other multi-segmented creatures here that have a very different way of life.

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:14.800
Centipedes.

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:17.630
They are meat eaters.

00:04:18.740 --> 00:04:24.000
Centipedes are closely related to millipedes, and they prey on other bugs.

00:04:25.340 --> 00:04:30.000
There are over 8,000 species of them, and they all have poisonous sting.

00:04:30.640 --> 00:04:33.540
This one lives in caves in India.

00:04:34.620 --> 00:04:40.020
Their powerful independently moving legs make them much faster than millipedes.

00:04:45.580 --> 00:04:50.190
This is the one to be feared most.

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:52.600
Meet Scolopendra.

00:05:09.950 --> 00:05:12.520
But it's not the only top predator here.

00:05:14.740 --> 00:05:21.860
This red-claw scorpion from Tanzania is not as fast or as agile,

00:05:21.950 --> 00:05:26.790
but its powerful pincers and sting make it every bit as deadly.

00:05:31.340 --> 00:05:33.920
Scorpions and centipedes are competitors.

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:44.440
They hunt the same prey in the same territory.

00:05:45.760 --> 00:05:49.750
And because of that, if they meet, they fight.

00:06:19.960 --> 00:06:25.300
Scolopendra uses all its strength to try and get under Red Claw's armor,

00:06:25.960 --> 00:06:28.960
but the Scorpion's defenses are too strong.

00:06:40.940 --> 00:06:44.640
It's not Red Claw's attack that wins the day,

00:06:44.640 --> 00:06:50.480
it's Scolopendra's weak defence that loses it.

00:06:50.480 --> 00:06:52.590
If you can't defend yourself,

00:06:52.590 --> 00:06:56.760
you're not likely to stay alive for long in the micro-world.

00:07:00.190 --> 00:07:05.590
Australia, home to a beetle with one of the oddest defences.

00:07:09.090 --> 00:07:13.340
This is a pie-dish beetle.

00:07:13.510 --> 00:07:16.210
The outback certainly gets very dry,

00:07:16.270 --> 00:07:20.890
so you might think that this strange shell is just for collecting water,

00:07:21.100 --> 00:07:25.150
but its primary function is defence.

00:07:27.640 --> 00:07:29.510
A mantis.

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:34.750
but with this potential prey, it doesn't know how to even begin.

00:07:34.830 --> 00:08:00.000
The pie-dish beetle's armor does have its disadvantages.

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:04.870
It's not what you might call manoeuvrable.

00:08:38.860 --> 00:08:49.060
Other Australian insects have a different defensive strategy.

00:08:49.060 --> 00:08:54.530
This stick insect relies on camouflage to make it invisible,

00:08:54.530 --> 00:08:57.310
but that too comes at a cost.

00:08:57.310 --> 00:09:00.000
Being shaped like a stick makes it difficult

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:05.250
to fly, so if a predator does find it, it could be done for.

00:09:33.840 --> 00:09:42.330
huntsman spider and it's ready to strike. It's one of the fastest and most agile of all spiders,

00:09:42.330 --> 00:09:56.470
but the stick insect doesn't even try to escape. She has a weapon of last resort,

00:10:03.800 --> 00:10:09.360
a milky substance from the glands behind her head.

00:10:13.040 --> 00:10:15.480
The spray can reach half a meter,

00:10:15.560 --> 00:10:20.990
and she'll have enough to have a few goes before running dry.

00:10:24.520 --> 00:10:27.650
It fills the forest with the scent of peppermint.

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:51.730
huntsman it's an unbearable irritant so the stick insect is free to graze in peace ever since they

00:10:51.730 --> 00:10:59.920
first appeared on land the arthropods have been fighting one another over food over territory over a mate

00:11:00.510 --> 00:11:07.540
and the ways they have developed in order to do so are truly astonishing.

00:11:10.190 --> 00:11:13.230
On the floor of forests almost anywhere in the world,

00:11:13.310 --> 00:11:20.910
you'll find these Bombardier beetles.

00:11:22.640 --> 00:11:24.390
They look fairly harmless,

00:11:24.480 --> 00:11:27.810
but not many creatures are foolhardy enough to try and eat them.

00:11:30.670 --> 00:11:36.850
Some mantises are so large and strong, they can even kill small birds.

00:11:36.850 --> 00:11:48.240
A beetle should be easy, but not this one.

00:11:48.240 --> 00:11:54.790
A fraction of a second after the mantis strikes,

00:11:54.790 --> 00:11:59.800
the beetle squirts hot gas and caustic chemicals in its face.

00:12:06.040 --> 00:12:09.080
The chemicals are produced by a reaction in its abdomen

00:12:09.160 --> 00:12:13.640
which generates enough heat to bring the liquid close to boiling point.

00:12:15.230 --> 00:12:25.670
The bombardier's spray can be deadly to smaller creatures,

00:12:25.980 --> 00:12:30.000
but the mantis is large and it survives.

00:12:32.490 --> 00:12:42.920
does the beetle. On the African savanna there's a hunter that sets its traps

00:12:42.920 --> 00:12:56.460
wherever there is dust or dry sand. This is the larva of an ant-lion. As an adult

00:12:56.460 --> 00:13:00.000
it will look something like a dragonfly and then its

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:06.650
sole purpose will be to mate. It'll barely even eat. But now, as a larva, its task is

00:13:06.650 --> 00:13:13.490
to feed and grow. And to do that, it digs a pit trap in the sand and buries itself at

00:13:13.490 --> 00:13:30.000
the bottom, jaws uppermost. It can jerk its head so violently that it can shoot up sand grains like

00:13:30.120 --> 00:13:50.870
bullets. Now it waits and wanders into the pit and loses its foothold.

00:14:00.190 --> 00:14:20.970
The ant can't climb out. The sand slips from under its feet. Eventually it tires and the antlion

00:14:20.970 --> 00:14:30.000
drags it beneath the sand and devours it. For the antlion, remaining hidden is a good

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:47.920
way to hunt, and there are a multitude of places to do that in a forest. Here, there

00:14:47.920 --> 00:14:55.430
are predators that set perhaps the most perfect of all ambushes.

00:14:55.430 --> 00:15:00.000
This female trapdoor spider lives in a burrow with the camouflage

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:18.260
lid, and she'll stay here for her entire 20-year life. Around the burrow, she's laid out a network

00:15:18.260 --> 00:15:27.180
of invisibly fine strands of silk. She'll pounce on anything that trips on them. Occasionally,

00:15:27.180 --> 00:15:30.000
she tests to see that her door opened.

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:30.640
smoothly.

00:15:33.140 --> 00:15:39.420
Mostly.

00:15:39.890 --> 00:15:40.570
She waits.

00:15:42.990 --> 00:15:49.230
A cricket. Got it.

00:16:04.990 --> 00:16:12.000
She pulls her prey into the burrow so that she can eat it in safety and at her leisure.

00:16:19.700 --> 00:16:23.730
But some bugs have such perfect camouflage,

00:16:24.030 --> 00:16:27.720
they're virtually invisible even though they're totally exposed.

00:16:34.640 --> 00:16:37.820
Predators use camouflage to hunt their prey unseen.

00:16:39.210 --> 00:16:42.360
Their victims use it to avoid being found and eaten.

00:16:43.680 --> 00:16:46.700
But disguise can be a potent weapon.

00:16:51.160 --> 00:17:00.000
In the forests of East Africa, one insect has taken its camouflage to a bizarre and somewhat gruesome extreme.

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:13.430
This is an assassin bug.

00:17:13.430 --> 00:17:19.690
To us, it's easy enough to spot because it moves to its prey.

00:17:19.690 --> 00:17:24.420
That's irrelevant because it smells like one of their number.

00:17:36.240 --> 00:17:41.830
The assassin sucks its victims dry and glues their empty husks onto its back.

00:17:42.440 --> 00:17:46.960
This one is already carrying at least 20 corpses.

00:18:05.210 --> 00:18:09.730
Its irregular shape makes it hard for other predators to spot it

00:18:09.730 --> 00:18:16.670
and makes it virtually invisible to its prey: ants.

00:18:16.670 --> 00:18:28.710
It enters this ant colony unchallenged.

00:18:32.300 --> 00:18:36.070
Its coat of ant corpses masks its own odour.

00:18:36.150 --> 00:18:39.320
To the ants, it smells like one of their own.

00:18:39.360 --> 00:18:40.980
And that's what matters.

00:18:42.200 --> 00:18:47.480
They'll even run straight over the top of it.

00:18:48.770 --> 00:18:58.100
The assassin simply takes an ant whenever it feels hungry.

00:19:03.640 --> 00:19:14.870
and the body of each victim then adds to its disguise.

00:19:14.870 --> 00:19:20.850
Conflict pervades the bug world.

00:19:20.850 --> 00:19:22.790
It shapes their bodies.

00:19:41.680 --> 00:19:43.350
It dictates their lifestyle.

00:19:44.220 --> 00:19:46.100
The ant-lions beneath the sand.

00:19:50.620 --> 00:19:51.920
The trapdoor spiders.

00:19:52.360 --> 00:19:53.700
Twenty years in a burrow.

00:20:08.030 --> 00:20:17.100
And it's their extraordinary diversity that underpins their success.

00:20:17.100 --> 00:20:24.540
In the next program, I'll look at arthropod predators.

00:20:24.540 --> 00:20:28.550
These are the creatures that must kill in order to eat.

00:20:31.850 --> 00:20:35.800
They have evolved the most extraordinary ways to do so.

00:20:35.800 --> 00:20:41.040
And they take many forms.

00:20:41.040 --> 00:20:54.280
We'll meet a spider that has turned its web into a casting net.

00:20:54.280 --> 00:21:00.000
We'll witness the ingenious hunting tactics of another spider that

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:08.000
on its fellows. We'll meet creatures that use the surface of water like a radar

00:21:08.000 --> 00:21:15.010
dish to detect their prey and an extraordinary wasp that tames a

00:21:15.010 --> 00:21:24.850
cockroach so her young can eat it alive. We'll encounter the most deadly

00:21:24.850 --> 00:21:28.800
creatures of the micro world.
